{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>He may not treat as firstborn the son of the loved wife ahead of the son of the unloved one, the firstborn.</b> He may not remove the birthright from the son on account of hatred for this one or love for that one. But if he does so on account of the wickedness of the firstborn son, then it is proper to remove it, as the Sages said (Bava Batra 133a): 'If he did not conduct himself properly — remember him for good.' And so it appears that Jacob our father did, as it says (1 Chronicles 5:1): וּבְחַלְּלוֹ יְצוּעֵי אָבִיו נִתְּנָה בְכֹרָתוֹ לִבְנֵי יוֹסֵף בֶּן יִשְׂרָאֵל ('and because he defiled his father's bed, his birthright was given to the sons of Joseph son of Israel')."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:21:57.535263934Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}